---
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import DocumentLayout from '../../layouts/DocumentLayout.astro';
import { profile } from '../../data/profile';
import ClickbaitAnalysisDiagram from '../../components/documents/ClickbaitAnalysisDiagram.astro';
import ClickbaitFlowDiagram from '../../components/documents/ClickbaitFlowDiagram.astro';
import architecture from '../../assets/portfolio/architecture.png';
import decomposition from '../../assets/portfolio/decomp.jpg';
import patch1 from '../../assets/portfolio/patch_1.jpg';
import patch3 from '../../assets/portfolio/patch_3.jpg';
import patch4 from '../../assets/portfolio/patch_4.jpg';
import patch5 from '../../assets/portfolio/patch_5.jpg';
import sceneBefore from '../../assets/portfolio/scene_before.jpg';
import sceneMid from '../../assets/portfolio/scene_mid.jpg';
import sceneAfter from '../../assets/portfolio/scene_after.jpg';
import seeker from '../../assets/portfolio/seeker.png';
import hover from '../../assets/portfolio/hover.png';
const blending = `prediction = cloudy * (1.0 - mask) + candidate * mask`;
---
<DocumentLayout locale="en" page="portfolio" title="SHIN DONGJU Portfolio" description="SHIN DONGJU's work on satellite image restoration models, shared training tools, and an article classification service.">
  <div class="sheet portfolio-sheet">
    <header class="portfolio-header">
      <div><h1>SHIN DONGJU</h1><p data-profile-role>{profile.role}</p></div>
      <address><a href="mailto:smturtle2@kakao.com">smturtle2@kakao.com</a><a href="https://github.com/smturtle2">github.com/smturtle2</a><a href="https://huggingface.co/Hermanni">huggingface.co/Hermanni</a></address>
    </header>

    <article class="portfolio-project">
      <header class="project-header">
        <div class="row"><h2><span class="entry-title">CLEAR-Net</span><span class="entry-desc">Cloud removal from SAR–optical satellite imagery</span></h2><p class="date">Mar 2026 – Jun 2026</p></div>
        <div class="row meta"><p>Dongguk University Capstone Design 1 · Team of 4 · Top Excellence Award</p><div class="links"><a href="https://github.com/smturtle2/cr-project">Model code</a><a href="https://huggingface.co/Hermanni/clear-net-sen12mscr">Model weights</a><a href="https://github.com/smturtle2/cr-train">Training package</a><a href="https://huggingface.co/datasets/Hermanni/sen12mscr-v2">Public dataset</a></div></div>
      </header>
      <p class="lead">Restoring cloud-obscured optical imagery while aiming to preserve the original input in clear regions</p>
      <ul class="work-list">
        <li>Optical imagery captures surface color and spectral information, but clouds hide parts of it</li>
        <li>SAR imagery is less affected by clouds and supplies terrain structure to help reconstruct a cloud-free optical image</li>
      </ul>
      <figure class="scene-sequence">
        <div><Image src={sceneBefore} alt="Optical input scene obscured by clouds" loading="eager" /><p>Cloudy optical input</p></div>
        <div><Image src={sceneMid} alt="Intermediate scene with some patches restored and others still cloudy" loading="eager" /><p>Patchwise progress</p></div>
        <div><Image src={sceneAfter} alt="Full-scene output assembled from CLEAR-Net patch predictions" loading="eager" /><p>CLEAR-Net output</p></div>
        <figcaption><a href="https://github.com/smturtle2/cr-project/blob/main/modules/util/clear_net_scene_demo.py">Full-scene inference demo</a> restoring 784 patches sequentially</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <section class="portfolio-case portfolio-case-long">
        <h3>Designing cloud removal around input preservation</h3>
        <section class="case-phase">
          <h4>01 Removing clouds while preserving clear input regions</h4>
          <ul class="work-list">
            <li>Ground truth (GT) is a cloud-free image acquired at another time; changes in the surface or observation conditions can make it differ from the input even in clear regions</li>
            <li>The goal was to remove clouds while retaining information already visible in the input, but ACA-CRNet adds a correction across the whole image and can also alter clear regions</li>
          </ul>
        </section>
        <section class="case-phase">
          <h4>02 Predicting the restoration candidate and mask separately</h4>
          <ul class="work-list">
            <li>Used ACA-CRNet, selected by the team for inference speed and training stability, as the restoration backbone and added a path that carries the original input to the output</li>
            <li>Predicted the restoration candidate separately from the mask that blends it with the input, so low mask values leave the input largely unchanged</li>
          </ul>
        </section>
        <section class="case-phase">
          <h4>03 Decomposing features by their role in restoration</h4>
          <ul class="work-list">
            <li>Designed, built, and compared four model architectures: LCR, FDT, FDT-CCA, and CLEAR-Net</li>
            <li>Replaced FDT's shared and sensor-specific decomposition with restoration and cloud components to align the split with what the candidate and mask each need</li>
            <li>Also considered possible optical bias in shared features (13 optical vs. 2 SAR channels) with a teammate when choosing the split</li>
          </ul>
          <figure class="diagram"><Image src={architecture} alt="CLEAR-Net architecture: a restoration path generates a candidate from SAR and optical features, while a separate mask path controls blending with the original input" loading="eager" /><figcaption>Restoration candidate path (bottom) and blending mask path (top)</figcaption></figure>
          <ul class="work-list">
            <li>Extracted the restoration component from combined SAR–optical features, then the cloud component from the remainder</li>
            <li>Fed the restoration component and dedicated SAR features to ACA-CRNet for the candidate; used the cloud component for the mask</li>
          </ul>
          <figure class="decomposition-figure"><Image src={decomposition} alt="Example intermediate features separated into restoration and cloud components, followed by candidate and mask generation" loading="eager" /><figcaption>Intermediate feature visualization for one input</figcaption></figure>
          <ul class="work-list">
            <li>Mixed 64 learned 13-band vectors with per-pixel weights from the cloud component to form the mask</li>
          </ul>
          <div class="model-blending">
            <p class="model-equation"><a href="https://github.com/smturtle2/cr-project/blob/decdb3342954d21ea8132efd6722b04fc33050f4/modules/model/CLEAR_Net/clear_net.py#L140"><code>{blending}</code></a></p>
            <p>For each pixel and band, a mask near 0 favors the input; a mask near 1 favors the candidate</p>
          </div>
          <ul class="work-list"><li>Supervised the candidate and auxiliary outputs to connect the decomposed features to restoration</li></ul>
          <table class="decision-table">
            <thead><tr><th>Training target</th><th>Loss</th><th>Purpose</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><th>Final output</th><td>L1+SSIM</td><td>Penalize pixel and structural differences</td></tr>
              <tr><th>Candidate / auxiliary</th><td>L1+SSIM</td><td>Supervise restoration before blending and in intermediate features</td></tr>
              <tr><th>Mask weights</th><td>Balancing</td><td>Discourage concentration on a few band vectors</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </section>
        <section class="case-phase">
          <h4>04 Full-scene restoration and comparison against GT</h4>
          <ul class="work-list">
            <li>Visualized the trained model's intermediate features and masks, and built a full-scene inference demo restoring 784 patches from a validation scene</li>
            <li>Against GT across whole images, CLEAR-Net achieved slightly higher SSIM than ACA-CRNet; ACA-CRNet performed better on MAE, PSNR, and SAM</li>
          </ul>
          <table class="metrics">
            <caption>SEN12MS-CR test set: 7,899 images · Trained for 120 epochs (~76 hours) on 4× A6000 with DDP and bf16</caption>
            <thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>MAE ↓</th><th>PSNR ↑</th><th>SSIM ↑</th><th>SAM ↓</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody><tr><th>ACA-CRNet</th><td><strong>0.0265</strong></td><td><strong>29.41</strong></td><td>0.8909</td><td><strong>7.910</strong></td></tr><tr><th>CLEAR-Net</th><td>0.0274</td><td>28.46</td><td class="accent"><strong>0.8936</strong></td><td>8.244</td></tr></tbody>
          </table>
          <figure class="restoration-comparison">
            <div><Image src={patch1} alt="Cloudy optical input" loading="eager" /><p>Optical input</p></div>
            <div><Image src={patch3} alt="ACA-CRNet restoration result" loading="eager" /><p>ACA-CRNet</p></div>
            <div class="selected"><Image src={patch4} alt="CLEAR-Net restoration result" loading="eager" /><p>CLEAR-Net</p></div>
            <div><Image src={patch5} alt="GT: cloud-free optical image acquired at another time" loading="eager" /><p>GT</p></div>
            <figcaption>A restoration example with more distinct river and road shapes than ACA-CRNet</figcaption>
          </figure>
        </section>
      </section>

      <section class="portfolio-case">
        <h3>Reducing attention memory use</h3>
        <div class="runtime-result attention-comparison">
        <div>
        <section class="case-phase">
          <h4>01 Over 6GB of VRAM for inference</h4>
          <ul class="work-list">
            <li>The feature-decomposition experiment used over 6GB of VRAM for inference alone</li>
            <li>Found that contextual attention stored all pairwise similarities, making memory grow quadratically with the number of positions</li>
          </ul>
        </section>
        <section class="case-phase">
          <h4>02 PyTorch SDPA for efficient attention</h4>
          <ul class="work-list"><li>Chose SDPA for compatibility with memory-efficient kernels such as FlashAttention, avoiding an explicit similarity matrix</li></ul>
        </section>
        <section class="case-phase">
          <h4>03 Rebuilding contextual attention with SDPA</h4>
          <ul class="work-list">
            <li>Reimplemented the operation with SDPA, measured before/after in the feature-decomposition experiment, and reused it in the final CLEAR-Net</li>
          </ul>
        </section>
        </div>
        <section class="case-phase">
          <h4>04 ~88% less VRAM and ~1.7× throughput on the experimental model</h4>
          <table class="compact-metrics">
            <thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>VRAM</th><th>Throughput</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody><tr><th>EMRDM (Diffusion)</th><td>2,782 MB</td><td>1.28 img/s</td></tr><tr><th>ACA-CRNet</th><td>6,264 MB</td><td>4.63 img/s</td></tr><tr><th>proposed</th><td><strong>724 MB</strong></td><td><strong>7.88 img/s</strong></td></tr></tbody>
          </table>
        </section>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="portfolio-case">
        <h3>Streaming a large training dataset</h3>
        <section class="case-phase">
          <h4>01 A 389GB dataset stored by scene</h4>
          <ul class="work-list">
            <li>The original 389GB dataset stored each scene as one parquet file (175 files averaging 2.2GB), making per-server or Colab copies impractical and mixing samples across scenes difficult</li>
          </ul>
        </section>
        <section class="case-phase">
          <h4>02 Small compressed blocks as the transfer unit</h4>
          <ul class="work-list">
            <li>Chose small compressed blocks over scene files to mix scenes without a full local copy</li>
          </ul>
          <table class="decision-table">
            <thead><tr><th>Approach</th><th>Selection and order</th><th>Local storage</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><th>HF streaming</th><td>Shuffle/termination issues in the initial implementation</td><td>No full copy needed</td></tr>
              <tr><th>Seeded block cache</th><td>Repeatable selection and order</td><td>Selected data copy</td></tr>
              <tr class="chosen"><th>Compressed blocks</th><td>Cross-scene mixing, fixed order</td><td>Needed blocks only</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </section>
        <section class="case-phase">
          <h4>03 Sample-based compression and a streaming loader</h4>
          <ul class="work-list">
            <li>Each sample pairs 256×256 SAR and cloudy optical input patches with cloud-free optical GT at the same location; packed up to 32 same-scene samples into each zstd <code>.crpack</code> block</li>
            <li>Built a seeded loader to mix samples across blocks, prefetch in parallel, and delete used blocks</li>
          </ul>
        </section>
        <section class="case-phase">
          <h4>04 Training across scenes with a small local footprint</h4>
          <ul class="work-list">
            <li>Republished 389GB as 240GB; trained on mixed-scene samples with about 5GB of local storage</li>
          </ul>
        </section>
      </section>

      <section class="portfolio-case">
        <h3>Separating model experiments from shared training code</h3>
        <section class="case-phase">
          <h4>01 Agent edits could change teammates' training conditions</h4>
          <ul class="work-list"><li>Everyone used coding agents with varying experience; agent edits to shared code could let one person's experiment alter teammates' training conditions</li></ul>
        </section>
        <section class="case-phase">
          <h4>02 A shared package and a separate model repository</h4>
          <ul class="work-list"><li>Packaged preprocessing, training, and evaluation; kept model and setting changes in <code>cr-project</code></li></ul>
        </section>
        <section class="case-phase">
          <h4>03 Responsibilities and change rules for cr-train and cr-project</h4>
          <ul class="work-list">
            <li>Owned <code>cr-train</code>: data loading, DDP training, evaluation, and metric logging in one package</li>
            <li>Managed shared code on main and experiments on branches; added a hook restricting shared runner-script commits to main</li>
          </ul>
        </section>
        <section class="case-phase">
          <h4>04 The same training and evaluation procedure across models</h4>
          <ul class="work-list">
            <li>Team members ran experiments on the shared package and compared four candidate models</li>
          </ul>
        </section>
      </section>
    </article>

    <article class="portfolio-project">
      <header class="project-header">
        <div class="row"><h2><span class="entry-title">Click-bait Seeker</span><span class="entry-desc">Clickbait classification for Chrome</span></h2><p class="date">Sep 2024 – Dec 2024</p></div>
        <div class="row meta"><p>Dongguk University Open Source Software Project · Team of 4</p><div class="links"><a href="https://github.com/CSID-DGU/2024-02-CSC4004-2-2-NewEngineeringCenterGhost">Project code</a><a href="https://chromewebstore.google.com/detail/blcclfjholbpgmmbnghodhlkagepjbbh">Chrome Web Store</a></div></div>
      </header>
      <p class="lead">A Chrome extension that pairs clickbait probability with key sentences to inspect and GPT commentary</p>
      <p>Hovering over a link shows its clickbait probability; analyzing an article page or selection shows the probability and GPT commentary and highlights key sentences in the article</p>
      <figure class="diagram"><ClickbaitAnalysisDiagram locale="en" /></figure>

      <section class="portfolio-case">
        <h3>Selecting key sentences with the article classifier</h3>
        <section class="case-phase">
          <h4>01 A probability alone does not show where to look</h4>
          <ul class="work-list"><li>Wanted to show sentences worth inspecting alongside the probability, but the training data had only article-level labels, making supervised sentence selection and evaluation difficult</li></ul>
        </section>
        <section class="case-phase">
          <h4>02 Reusing the classifier's layer attention</h4>
          <ul class="work-list">
            <li>Used attention from the trained Transformer classifier to select sentences without sentence labels</li>
            <li>Considered final-layer attention and attention rollout, which combines attention across layers</li>
          </ul>
        </section>
        <section class="case-phase">
          <h4>03 Exposing layer attention and comparing sentence selections</h4>
          <ul class="work-list">
            <li>Owned article preprocessing and classifier training; joined titles, subtitles, and bodies from about 730K AI Hub articles with SEP and trained with inputs of up to 3,584 tokens</li>
            <li>Modified the trained classifier's TransformerEncoder to return layer attention; read and compared sentences selected by final-layer attention and rollout, then adopted rollout</li>
            <li>Returned sentences containing high-scoring tokens, capping selection at five tokens to avoid excessive highlighting</li>
          </ul>
        </section>
        <section class="case-phase">
          <h4>04 Returning a probability and key sentences together</h4>
          <ul class="work-list">
            <li>The model trained on article-level clickbait labels supplied both a classification probability and key sentences to inspect</li>
          </ul>
        </section>
      </section>

      <section class="portfolio-case">
        <h3>Learning jamo embeddings to handle Korean typos</h3>
        <section class="case-phase">
          <h4>01 Typos change syllables and word representations</h4>
          <ul class="work-list"><li>Typos such as ‘스맡폰’ and ‘ㅏㅣ폰’ share few syllable-level pieces with their intended words, making it difficult to obtain similar word representations</li></ul>
        </section>
        <section class="case-phase">
          <h4>02 Combining jamo decomposition with FastText subwords</h4>
          <ul class="work-list"><li>Chose jamo decomposition with FastText's substring-based embeddings because splitting Korean into consonants and vowels preserves shared pieces even when syllables change</li></ul>
        </section>
        <section class="case-phase">
          <h4>03 Training embeddings on a separate Korean corpus</h4>
          <ul class="work-list">
            <li>Owned embedding training: decomposed a separate Korean corpus into jamo and trained FastText with Gensim</li>
            <li>Used the embeddings as classifier inputs and compared nearest words for the same typos with and without jamo decomposition</li>
          </ul>
        </section>
        <section class="case-phase">
          <h4>04 Intended words ranked highly for three example typos</h4>
          <ul class="work-list"><li>All three example typos had unrelated nearest words before decomposition; afterward, intended words such as ‘스마트폰’ (smartphone), ‘아이폰’ (iPhone), and ‘햄버거’ (hamburger) ranked near the top</li></ul>
          <table class="typo">
            <caption>Nearest words (cosine similarity)</caption>
            <thead><tr><th>Input</th><th>Without jamo decomposition</th><th>With jamo decomposition</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody><tr><th>스맡폰</th><td>이 0.20 · 증기 0.20</td><td><strong>스마트 0.84 · 스마트폰 0.81</strong></td></tr><tr><th>ㅏㅣ폰</th><td>매료 0.19 · 베이지 0.18</td><td><strong>아이폰 0.83</strong> · 폰 0.81</td></tr><tr><th>햄벅</th><td>해질 0.21 · 낙관 0.20</td><td><strong>햄버거 0.86</strong> · 핫도그 0.68</td></tr></tbody>
          </table>
        </section>
      </section>

      <section class="portfolio-case portfolio-case-long">
        <div class="case-opening">
          <h3>Connecting Python inference to the article interface</h3>
        <section class="case-phase">
          <h4>01 Placeholder probabilities close to the deadline</h4>
          <ul class="work-list"><li>By late November, model training was nearly complete, but the extension still showed placeholder probabilities; the Python model, Spring Boot server, and extension had yet to be connected through real responses</li></ul>
        </section>
        <section class="case-phase">
          <h4>02 Keeping the model loaded in a persistent Python process</h4>
          <ul class="work-list">
            <li>Kept the model in a persistent Python process to avoid reloading it for each request; connected a teammate's Spring Boot server to that process through a request helper and Unix socket</li>
          </ul>
        </section>
        </div>
        <div class="case-resolution">
        <section class="case-phase">
          <h4>03 Building inference and taking on frontend integration</h4>
          <ul class="work-list"><li>Built the Python inference server, then took on result display and GPT commentary integration late in the project, using a coding agent to understand and work with the existing frontend</li></ul>
          <figure class="diagram"><ClickbaitFlowDiagram locale="en" /><figcaption>Highlighted: parts I connected · Spring Boot: teammate</figcaption></figure>
          <ul class="work-list">
            <li>Implemented per-link caching, a request queue, and duplicate-request suppression to reduce repeated analysis and requests for the same link</li>
            <li>Matched extracted sentences, which lost spaces when rebuilt from tokens, against the space-stripped body using longest common substring (LCS), then mapped matches back to the original text for highlighting</li>
            <li>Connected the article interface to display probability and commentary generated by the team backend, which sent the article body and key sentences to <code>gpt-4o-mini</code></li>
          </ul>
        </section>
        <section class="case-phase">
          <div class="outcome-preview">
            <div class="outcome-copy">
              <h4>04 Live analysis results and Chrome Web Store release</h4>
              <ul class="work-list"><li>Replaced placeholder probabilities with real server responses, completing probability display, sentence highlighting, and GPT commentary; the team released the extension on the Chrome Web Store</li></ul>
            </div>
            <figure class="hover-preview"><Image src={hover} alt="Clickbait probability displayed when hovering over an article link" loading="eager" format="png" /></figure>
          </div>
          <figure class="product-screen"><Image src={seeker} alt="Article interface with a clickbait probability and GPT commentary banner above highlighted key sentences" loading="eager" format="png" /></figure>
        </section>
        </div>
      </section>
    </article>
  </div>
</DocumentLayout>
